#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# polynomial_gcd

#% text
# `polynomial_gcd(p, q, ...)` gives the greatest common divisor of the polynomials.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The gcd is taken over the rationals in all variables of the arguments and normalized to a positive leading coefficient; integer content is included.

#% item
# `modulus: p` (a prime) gives the monic gcd over the integers modulo p, for polynomials in one variable; `trig: true` expands trigonometric functions and writes even powers of `cos(u)` through `sin(u)` first; `extension: a` works over the numbers generated by `a` (`automatic`: the algebraic numbers in the coefficients).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
polynomial_gcd(x^2 - 1, x^2 - 2*x + 1)
#% output
#= -1 + x

#% cell
polynomial_gcd(x^3 - x, x^2 + x)
#% output
#= x + x^2

#% cell
polynomial_gcd(6*x^2 + 6*x, 4*x + 4)
#% output
#= 2 + 2*x

#% cell
polynomial_gcd(x^2 + 1, x - 1)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several variables:

#% cell
polynomial_gcd(x^2 - y^2, x^2 + 2*x*y + y^2)
#% output
#= x + y

#% text
# More than two polynomials:

#% cell
polynomial_gcd(x^4 - 1, x^3 - x, x^2 - 1)
#% output
#= -1 + x^2

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Cancelling a common factor of a fraction:

#% cell
p = x^3 - 1; q = x^2 - 1; g = polynomial_gcd(p, q); [cancel(p/g), cancel(q/g)]
#% output
#= [1 + x + x^2, 1 + x]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `gcd*lcm` is the product, up to a constant:

#% cell
p = x^2 - 1; q = x^2 + 2*x + 1; expand(polynomial_gcd(p, q)*polynomial_lcm(p, q) - p*q)
#% output
#= 0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `polynomial_lcm`, `factor`, `cancel`, `gcd`, `resultant`
